In article <9005050446.AA26686@cie.uoregon.edu> ghudson@CIE.UOREGON.EDU writes:
>1. The weapons and named weapons tables refer to an "Sdam" and an "Ldam."

1) Weapons do sdam to small monsters and ldam to large monsters.  The damage
is in a die of the appropriate size.  For instance, an 8 means one eight-sided
die (a random number from 1 to 8).  (This refers to the unnamed weapons found
in objects.c).

The +whatever in the comments indicates that more damage is done.  For
instance, a weapon might do 2-7 points of damage.  This is calculated by
taking the result of a 6-sided die (1-6) and adding a constant 1.  objects.c
just lists the 6, and the additional 1 is added on manually in weapon.c.  The
comment is just so that you don't have to look in weapon.c if you're wondering
how much damage something does.

All weapons which exist in AD&D do exactly the same amount of damage as they
do in AD&D.  (AD&D also has small and large weapon damage, too.)

2) The named weapons in artifact.c.  This is coded a bit badly.  The data
structure used is the same one as used for monster attacks, although it is
inappropriate to do so (the numbers having different interpretations than
for monster attacks).  

{ 0, AT_PHYS, 3, 12 }

is what Cleaver has, for instance.  

The first 0 is never used (as I said, the data structure used here is an
inappropriate one).  The second indicates what type of attack the weapon has
(physical, in this case).  The 3 indicates that the weapon has a 1-3 bonus
to hit (rolled each round).  The 12 indicates that it has a 1-12 bonus to its
damage (rolled each round).  If the 3 were a zero instead, it would mean no
attack bonus, but if the 12 were a zero instead, it would mean double damage.

Some weapons have a second structure, of similar format.  The "attack type"
in there tells what the weapon gives resistance against.  The other three
fields in this structure all have zeroes--they're not used for anything.
(See above comment about inappropriate structures.)

Also, some weapons are only good against certain types of creatures, indicated
in the first line.
